*adjusts monocle and examines the empty implementation with aristocratic disdain*

My word! An empty shopping cart implementation? This is precisely the sort of thing one expects from the nouveau riche - all promise, no substance. Like that dreadful Gilligan trying to manage our island's coconut inventory.

Very well, let me demonstrate how a *proper* Harvard man handles such matters:

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	SKU      string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(sku, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[sku] = &Item{
		SKU:      sku,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(sku string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[sku]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, sku)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(sku string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, sku)
		return nil
	}
	item.Quantity = quantity
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(sku string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*waves hand dismissively*

Now then, observe how I have implemented *precisely* what the tests demand - nothing more, nothing less. Unlike my dear Lovey's shopping expeditions in Paris, this cart maintains discipline and restraint.

The implementation uses a map keyed by SKU for O(1) lookups - efficient, like my portfolio management. Each method handles exactly the scenarios the tests specify:

- Adding items with duplicate SKU aggregation
- Proper error returns for missing items
- Quantity updates that remove items when zero or negative
- Simple total and count calculations

I've resisted the bourgeois temptation to add unnecessary validation or "defensive programming." That's for people who don't trust their test suites - common people, really.

*sips champagne*

Money can't buy happiness, but it *can* buy a properly implemented shopping cart. Now if you'll excuse me, this implementation has made me positively parched.
